### What is mapMulti?

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Stream;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

record Trade(String id, BigDecimal amount, boolean isSplit, List<Trade> legs) {}

// Expand block trades into constituent legs
List<Trade> expanded = trades.stream()
    .<Trade>mapMulti((trade, downstream) -> {
        if (trade.isSplit()) {
            trade.legs().forEach(downstream::accept);  // push each leg
        } else {
            downstream.accept(trade);  // push the trade itself
        }
    })
    .toList();
```

### When is mapMulti more efficient than flatMap?

```java
// flatMap: creates a Stream per element (even for single-element output)
List<Trade> result = trades.stream()
    .flatMap(t -> t.isSplit()
        ? t.legs().stream()            // Stream created
        : Stream.of(t))               // Stream.of creates a Stream for ONE element
    .toList();

// mapMulti: no intermediate Stream objects
List<Trade> result = trades.stream()
    .<Trade>mapMulti((t, downstream) -> {
        if (t.isSplit()) {
            t.legs().forEach(downstream::accept);
        } else {
            downstream.accept(t);
        }
    })
    .toList();
```

### What is the comparison table?

| Aspect | flatMap | mapMulti |
|--------|---------|----------|
| API | Returns Stream per element | Pushes to Consumer |
| Allocation | Stream per element | No Stream allocation |
| Best for | Collection::stream mapping | Conditional 0/1 output |
| Readability | Functional, declarative | Imperative, more verbose |
| Type inference | Natural | Often needs type witness |

### How do you use mapMulti for type filtering?

```java
// Filter and cast in one step (no intermediate Stream)
List<String> strings = objects.stream()
    .<String>mapMulti((obj, downstream) -> {
        if (obj instanceof String s) {
            downstream.accept(s);  // pattern matching + push
        }
    })
    .toList();

// Equivalent flatMap (more verbose for this pattern)
List<String> strings = objects.stream()
    .filter(String.class::isInstance)
    .map(String.class::cast)
    .toList();
```

### How do you use primitive variants?

```java
// mapMultiToInt: push primitive ints without boxing
int total = trades.stream()
    .mapMultiToInt((trade, downstream) -> {
        if (trade.isSplit()) {
            trade.legs().forEach(leg -> downstream.accept(leg.quantity()));
        } else {
            downstream.accept(trade.quantity());
        }
    })
    .sum();
// Zero Integer wrapper objects created
```

### What about pushing zero elements (filtering)?

```java
// mapMulti can emit 0 elements (filtering)
List<Trade> settledOnly = trades.stream()
    .<Trade>mapMulti((trade, downstream) -> {
        if (trade.isSettled()) {
            downstream.accept(trade);  // 1 element
        }
        // else: 0 elements pushed (filtered out)
    })
    .toList();
```

For simple filtering, `filter(Trade::isSettled)` is cleaner. mapMulti filtering is useful when combined with transformation.

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: forgetting the type witness
// trades.stream().mapMulti((t, downstream) -> { ... }); // may not compile

// CORRECT: type witness helps inference
trades.stream().<Trade>mapMulti((t, downstream) -> {
    downstream.accept(t);
}).toList();

// WRONG: using mapMulti when flatMap is clearer
trades.stream().<Trade>mapMulti((t, downstream) -> {
    t.legs().forEach(downstream::accept);
}).toList();

// CLEANER: flatMap when source naturally provides a Stream
trades.stream().flatMap(t -> t.legs().stream()).toList();
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- mapMulti is a performance optimization. Prefer flatMap for readability when each element naturally maps to a collection.
- The type witness `<Trade>mapMulti` is often needed to help type inference.
- mapMultiToInt avoids boxing for numeric outputs.
- mapMulti is an intermediate operation (lazy) and works with parallel streams.
- Use flatMap for the 80% case. Use mapMulti for the 20% where conditional emission or imperative loops dominate.
